vulkan-game

Game code is organized by namespace. Each namespace has one header,
including its types, declarations and implementations:

  server/scene.hpp           scene
  server/weapon.hpp          weapon
  server/player.hpp          player
  server/net/*.hpp           net::*
  server/net/movement/detail.hpp
  server/srv/*.hpp           srv::*
  server/game/*.hpp          game::*
  server/game_weapons.hpp    game_weapons
  client/clt/*.hpp           clt::*

Only server/server.cpp and client/client.cpp are application translation
units. They contain the entry points and host setup; the client includes
the shared server code with VULKAN_GAME_SHARED_ONLY. Assets and shaders
stay in their existing root directories.

Dependencies: VulkanWrapper, VulkanSystems and Vulkan-Headers submodules,
GLFW, fastgltf, Ozz, stb and Mbed TLS.

Setup:
  git submodule update --init --recursive
  c build vulkan-server
  c build vulkan-client

Run from the repository root:
  c build run vulkan-server
  c build run vulkan-client

The namespace-header layout has not yet been validated by a native build.

Mirror performance profiling (same camera, resolution and scene each run):
  c build run vulkan-server
  c build run vulkan-client
  OPTICS_PROFILE_GENERAL=1 c build run vulkan-client
  OPTICS_PROFILE_DISABLE=1 c build run vulkan-client

Compare 'present_p50' and 'present_p95' from at least 120 completed
frames, not 'optics_inner_stamp'. The default build selects a smaller
mirror-only fragment shader for scenes without any glass, eliminating
the full 16-entry glass-ray work queue. OPTICS_PROFILE_GENERAL forces
the original generic shader for a same-image A/B benchmark; when the
scene contains glass the full shader is always used. On tile-based GPUs (especially Apple
Metal/MoltenVK), timestamps inside the presentation render pass may not
isolate its fragment shading; 'present' includes the base fullscreen
draw, optical overlay and editor. The profile-disable option is an
intentional A/B diagnostic and does not affect default rendering.
The optics coverage log counts submitted conservative scissor pixels;
it is not a count of actual mirror-mask fragments. Disjoint rectangles
remove duplicate shading without changing the image. Closest-hit and
shadow traversal use exact BVH triangles, compact face IDs and per-ray
precomputed reciprocal directions. Perfect mirrors use an exact
serial mirror path and retain the full tracer when glass is reached.
No resolution scaling or denoising is used. Native GPU timings are
necessary to quantify any improvement.
